ack won by 10 points. Both sides traded the lead through the middle game: after White took a 4.6-point lead on move 15, Opus 5.5 gave it straight back with three costly moves (turns 16, 18 and 20). From there Black's lead never fell below 6.4 points, and by the final passes it stood at 10.4 before komi was settled into the result. Opus 5.5 averaged 2.2 points lost per move to the 12k's 1.8.", "moments": [{"turn": 8, "text": "The 12k's D5 on turn 7 lost 4.7 points and handed White the lead at -4.7. D6 lost 5.9 points, the engine preferred E5, and the lead went back to Black at +1.2. The first chance to pull ahead slipped away."}, {"turn": 16, "text": "Opus 5.5's worst move. After Black's D3 (5.8 points lost) White led by 4.6, and B4 threw it away: 9.6 points lost, the lead swung from -4.6 to +5.0 for Black. The engine's choice was F6, the point Black took itself three turns later."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "Black's E8 lost 7.7 points and put White back in front at -2.7. B6 gave 7.2 points straight back, and Black led by 4.5 again. Both sides traded big errors here, but White's came second and left the board as Black's."}, {"turn": 20, "text": "A5 lost another 6.9 points, taking the lead from +4.8 to +11.7 for Black. The engine wanted C3, which Black then played on turn 23. This was Opus 5.5's third blunder, and it was the last time the game was really open."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "On the right side White's H6, H7 and J8 each lost about 5 points (5.0, 4.9, 4.7), while Black's H5, H8 and J6 each lost about 5 too. The lead bounced between +6.4 and +11.4 but ended each exchange where it began. The engine kept pointing at D1 for White, and the gap was settled elsewhere."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py plays one move the player names and reports captures and group liberties, libs.py lists group liberties, and count.py scores territory with dead stones the player lists by hand. None of them loops over candidate moves or looks ahead,